WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:03.020
Just keep on building, keep on growing.

2
00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:08.900
[Music]

3
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:12.980
My dad, he had family friends
who were dentists. They're brothers.

4
00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:14.500
They kind of took me under their wing,

5
00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.300
and when I didn't really know
what I wanted to do,

6
00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:17.700
they gave me a job at their office,

7
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:20.600
and I did some assisting
and sterilizing instruments.

8
00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:24.220
And what I noticed most that I liked
was just the team dynamic

9
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.460
and the patient relationships
that all the staff had.

10
00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.450
That's what really drew me into dentistry.

11
00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.030
They walk in, they know who they are.

12
00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:33.510
Ask them about their families and whatnot.

13
00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:36.790
You could be a kid going to dentists
all the way to an adult,

14
00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.640
and you kind of grow up together.

15
00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.630
That's like your work family,
your second family.

16
00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.520
That family is important to me.

17
00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:48.530
Friends on that journey,
you kind of struggle together.

18
00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:50.860
It's always nice.
It's hard to struggle alone.

19
00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:53.650
If you're gonna struggle, at least
you'll struggle with someone else,

20
00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.860
and then you help each other
out along the way.

21
00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.830
I happened to meet Dr. Hamamoto,

22
00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:03.530
the previous practice owner,
at a study group.

23
00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:06.030
She offered to sell the practice
because it was a good fit,

24
00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:09.400
personality-wise
and dental philosophy-wise, as well.

25
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.540
She tells me that my demeanor
is similar to her predecessor,

26
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.040
Dr. Benson Kanemoto, before her,
who she took over the practice from.

27
00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:18.620
So this practice does have
a lot of history,

28
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:19.860
and a lot of patients are still around,

29
00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:21.880
and it's almost kind of
full circle and nice,

30
00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:25.470
where the changing of the guard
is a smooth transition.

31
00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:28.990
She's like an auntie to me now,
and like her nephew.

32
00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:31.180
It's going back to
the whole family dynamic.

33
00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:36.830
That can be scary for patients
when someone else new comes in.

34
00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:39.230
Just having them get a chance to stay

35
00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:42.220
and then getting Kris's blessing,

36
00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:45.370
and then having them just get
a chance to meet me,

37
00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.640
it kind of helps with that
fear of the unknown.

38
00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:53.270
Just making sure the patient experience
is still good

39
00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:55.610
and consistent on what Dr. Hamamoto built,

40
00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:57.760
and kind of building off of that.

41
00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.160
The staff has been the same staff
that she had,

42
00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.020
and they've been great
and supportive as well.

43
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.430
Can't do it without them.

44
00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.290
Just keep on building, keep on growing.

45
00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:13.600
[Music]
